 roberts who served in the  state assembly after his career at metro  ended now lombardo has endorsed mcmahill  but also has had said some nice things  about roberts so far john langler talked  to both of them this week so let's start  with kevin mcmahill and his answer to  addressing a rise in violent crime  there's three sort of unsolvable issues  at this point that all of government is  approaching right it's the homeless  issue the addiction issue and the mental  health issue and you know today  unfortunately the clark county detention  center remains the largest addiction  treatment center in the state of nevada  it's also the largest mental health  facility in the state of nevada and we  probably house more homeless in the  clark county detention center than  anywhere else and so  the other challenge that i have to tell  you with that is is typically one or all  three of those things are involved  oftentimes in the challenges that our  officers have that will ultimately end  up in an officer-involved shooting so  they're deploying crisis response teams  right now as we speak at metro that's a  licensed clinical social worker along  with a paramedic and an emt that go out  to these calls for service they're  handling about six of them per day  they're only taking place at the  convention center area command and the  downtown area command  we have 11 area commands i think it's  important that we continue to  utilize those those types of resources  to assist us sheriff lombardo now  running for governor has already sort of  supported you and your campaign to uh to  become the sheriff what of  sheriff lombardo's term as as sheriff  and your own time at metro would you  want to continue and what do you think  needs to be changed in another  administration yes so i actually have  the support of the last three sheriffs  sheriff young sheriff gillespie and of  course sheriff lombardo and i i think um  you know we've always been very  fortunate to have very good sheriffs  here um  i think that one of the things that i  certainly continue on is is that metro  has been very exceptional at crime  fighting  that's what they want from the community  wants from their police department they  want that safety and security piece of  it  one of the things that i can tell you  that i bring to the table that  that we're going to continue to improve  upon and and you know i think it's a  direct result of of some of the  challenges that all police departments  have across this  uh nation of ours is that the community  doesn't really feel connected oftentimes  to the police department and i i think  that we're better at it than most but  one of the things that i'm really going  to focus in on is that sort of authentic  community relationship building  so that we can better understand one  another in in times of crisis and we  don't have to have the challenges that  we've seen in the years past use of  force and oversight that was addressed  in the last legislative session the  attorney general's office uh being  involved now and reviewing use of force  what involvement  do you believe on an on another agency  level on the state level whatever the  case may be should metro police have for  use of force  well i think metro is a model for the  use of force piece of it i had a big  hand in creating the current model that  exists at metro not only from a  administrative investigation which looks  at policy procedure training tactics  leadership supervision of all of the  officers involved in that incident  but you know i welcome that additional  scrutiny i really do because  most people don't know i know we just  had another officer-involved shooting i  believe it was yesterday that's number  six for metro  in 2010 when we had the department of  justice come in we had 25 and so metro  is making the right steps but we still  have to find those alternatives to  deadly force and continue to train that  into our officers you're running against  tom robe