y and the  folks that we do police  more so i think we do a decent job of it  i think we could do much better  i talked about our schools and our  seniors is actually involving them in  the policing process and telling us  where we need to focus our attention on  i think it's leveraging public-private  partnerships with the business community  and actually putting cops where they  need to be and putting them in our  neighborhoods they do these uh the first  tuesdays i think it is where they go out  into the different commands and there's  sort of events with the community  are you referring to something more than  that something more  robust than that in terms of community  and if so  how would it look so i mean we have a  comstat process which is we go over our  crime statistics on on a weekly basis  actually every morning there's a crime  call they go over go over call  go over crime that occurred in the last  24 hours but i think you should open  that up to the public and you should  have  those meetings in your community so you  can get actual real-time feedback not  only from your police officers that that  patrol the streets but you're the you  know the community that you serve one of  the issues that was brought up in the  legislative session uh in the past which  you obviously you were at dealing with  investigation of use of force and  involving the attorney general's office  in that  what is your position on that should  metro essentially investigate itself in  the use of deadly force or do you  subscribe to the idea that an outside  agency  should be responsible for that well i  believe now look i was part of that  process over the last five or six years  i think we do a very good job  of investigating our own use of force i  think it's important to have an outside  entity that would be able to audit that  or to take a look at that i think we  found several years ago when we added  the citizens review board for internal  affairs cases that our level of  i