fs  here um  i think that one of the things that i  certainly continue on is is that metro  has been very exceptional at crime  fighting  that's what they want from the community  wants from their police department they  want that safety and security piece of  it  one of the things that i can tell you  that i bring to the table that  that we're going to continue to improve  upon and and you know i think it's a  direct result of of some of the  challenges that all police departments  have across this  uh nation of ours is that the community  doesn't really feel connected oftentimes  to the police department and i i think  that we're better at it than most but  one of the things that i'm really going  to focus in on is that sort of authentic  community relationship building  so that we can better understand one  another in in times of crisis and we  don't have to have the challenges that  we've seen in the years past use of  force and oversight that was addressed  in the last legislative session the  attorney general's office uh being  involved now and reviewing use of force  what involvement  do you believe on an on another agency  level on the state level whatever the  case may be should metro police have for  use of force  well i think metro is a model for the  use of force piece of it i had a big  hand in creating the current model that  exists at metro not only from a  administrative investigation which looks  at policy procedure training tactics  leadership supervision of all of the  officers involved in that incident  but you know i welcome that additional  scrutiny i really do because  most people don't know i know we just  had another officer-involved shooting i  believe it was yesterday that's number  six for metro  in 2010 when we had the department of  justice come in we had 25 and so metro  is making the right steps but we still  have to find those alternatives to  deadly force and continue to train that  into our officers you're running against  tom roberts a man i'm sure you're  familiar with obviously he had a long  service with metro police as well  how  would you say you are different than  what he might bring to metro police  listen i'm not going to sit here and say  one bad word to you about tom roberts  tom did an exceptional job while he  worked for me as an assistant sheriff he  did everything i ever asked him to do  tom's a good man i just believe i'm more  qualified than tom you know everything  from the the bundy incident to uh being  in charge of one october to the uh covid  response and then obviously the summer  of unrest and rioting that we had i was  out there on the front lines with our  troops  and i just believe that my education and  experience  puts me a notch above tom and that's why  i'm running for sheriff  kevin mcmahill there now his opponent as  you just heard is tom roberts who also  spent time in the legislature here's  john's interview with him  simply put what changes would you make  if elected as sheriff from the lombardo  administration or do you think things  are moving at a reasonably good  motion right now  well look crime is up uh you know basic  all crime is up five percent this year  homicides are up 60 something percent  vehicle theft up 33 percent and i think  that we need to stay focused or my top  priority would be making las vegas the  safest  community in america and i would focus  on the basics right is  our most vulnerable population our  seniors and children and obviously our  economic engine the strip and our  tourist corridor so what's the solution  to that frequently i think a lot in law  enforcement even the sheriff  has said you know the issue would be  come down to funding or manpower  resources and so forth if elected  sheriff what's the solution to  the  spike in murders the spike in violent  crime a lot of these horrible things  that we've been seeing in our community  well i think it's a matter of changing  the way we police  in that we involve the community and the  folks that we do police  more so i think we do a decent job of it  i think we could do much better  i talked about our schools and our  seniors is actually involving them in  the policing process and telling us  where we need to focus our attention on  i think it's leveraging public-private  partnerships with the business community  and actually putting cops where they  need to be and putting them in our  neighborhoods they do these uh the first  tuesdays i think it is where they go out  into the different commands and there's  sort of events with the community  are you referring to something more than  that something more  robust than that in terms of community  and if so  how would it look so i mean we have a  comstat process which is we go over our  crime statistics on on a weekly basis  actually every morning there's a crime  call they go over go over call  go over crime that occurred in the last  24 hours but i think you should open  that up to the public and you should  have  those meetings in your community so you  can get actual real-time feedback not  only from your police officers that that  patrol the streets but you're the you  know the community that you serve one of  the issues that was brought up in the  legislative session uh in the past which  you obviously you were at dealing with  investigation of use of force and  involving the attorney general's office  in that  what is your position on that should  metro essentially investigate itself in  the use of deadly force or do you  subscribe to the idea that an outside  agency  should be responsible for that well i  believe now look i was part of that  process over the last five or six years  i think we do a very good job  of investigating our own use of force i  think it's important to have an outside  entity that would be able to audit that  or to take a look at that i think we  found several years ago when we added  the citizens review board for internal  affairs cases that our level of  investigations went up anytime you have  somebody looking at those things i think  you do a much better job and i i don't  believe that we at metro would be afraid  of that outside scrutiny and i think we  would welcome it what are the  differences between yourself and mr  mcmahon well look i think  we both have a lot of uh experience i've  got over 10 years experience in  executive staff he's got some experience  there i think some of the fundamental  differences  are from an education standpoint uh you  know i have a master's degree he doesn't  i have the highest level of post  certification  that you can have in the state he does  not i think our leadership style  varies somewhat and you'll see the  differences as this campaign continues  to roll out well tell me about your  leadership style look i believe in  collaboration and working with others i  believe in being approachable  not closed door i think  officers and should be able to get to  the fifth floor at metro right now they  can't they have to make an appointment  to get up there i think the sheriff  needs to be out in the public and i  think the sheriff needs to be out with  the troops  now both roberts and mcmahon had a lot  to say about crime fighting strategies  as well they had positive things to say  about the overall direction at metro you  can watch those full interviews on that  and what their code policies would be  for the department over on eight newsnow  dot com well a government shutdown is  looming up next alex limon explains what  budget reconciliation is  right now thousands of migrants mostly  from haiti are living under a bridge in  del rio texas at one point there were as  many as fifteen thousand people there  the body administration facing pressure  from all sides over the deteriorating  situation republicans complaining about  haitian migrants being released here  into the u.s democrats though saying the  conditions are just simply inhumane well  the federal government is now a week  from a shutdown if democrats and  republicans can't agree on a path  forward to fund our government right now  the two sides sparring over a democratic  push to attach a debt ceiling height to  that 3.5 trillion dollar spending plan  republicans are refusing to support that  effort  if they want to tax borrow and spend  historic sums of money without our input  they'll have to raise the debt limit  without our help i didn't want to vote  for it when trump was president but i  did because my obligation is to pay our  bills so that we can make veterans va  benefit payments so we can make social  security payment now the house already  passed a bill to fund the government and  raise the debt ceiling but democrats  need at least 10 republicans to pass the  plan over in the senate unless they  managed to use what is called budget  reconciliation  john langler talked with our washington  d.c correspondent alexandra limon to  explain exactly what that means  democrats are hoping to pass that 3.5  trillion dollar spending plan using  budget reconciliation it's a term we've  heard a lot about lately but  it's pretty technical alex simone  joining us from washington to to talk  about it and what this budget  reconciliation what it really is alex  hey there well budget reconciliation is  a process by which lawmakers try to pass  a budget to put it simply now in the u.s  house lawmakers always only need a  simple majority in order to pass a piece  of legislation but that is not the case  in the senate because of the filibuster  senators need to have 60 senators  support  a piece of legislation in order to pass  it the budget reconciliation however  provides an exemption to that rule any  bill that or any package that is passed  under this process of budget  reconciliation can be passed with only a  simple majority and that is why both  parties really try to use that process  when they can to try to pass major  pieces of legislation republicans for  example use that process to pass their  tax cuts which also provided you know  big tax cuts to corporations they  slashed the corporate tax rate and now  democrats are trying to do the same  thing trying to approve their spending  plan plan 3.5 trillion dollars that  would invest in things like free  preschool community college and also  make big investments in green energy to  try to fight climate change they too are  trying to pass it under budget  reconciliation which again would only  require a simple majority in the senate  so alex again as you said it's a  technical uh situation there in capitol  hill is a technical process only  requires that simple majority the  democrats there's a big push to get it  done but they still haven't gotten it  done where do things stand right now  with getting this all approved and  pushing it through  yeah although democrats have the  majority in the senate they've still not  been able to reach an agreement within  their own party in order to actually get  that done and get that budget  reconciliation package passed and that's  because there's some disagreement some  pretty big disagreement between the more  progressive members of the caucus and  then the more moderate members of the  caucus like joe manchin and kristin  cinema for example who don't agree with  various different things the large price  tag of it for example 3.5 3.5 trillion  dollars but also some of the other  measures uh west virginia obviously  being a big coal country area so joe  manchin perhaps pushing back on some of  those green energy initiatives and what  that would do for the coal industry in  his state  so we're seeing a lot of disagreement  and so far democrats have just had  trouble getting all of the members of  their own party in the senate to agree  and that was alex limon from washington  day marking four years since 1 october  in what to watch tonight there will be a  remembrance over at the healing garden  we will be covering that on 1 october  all day long here at channel 8. and as  alex just mentioned the budget runs out  tuesday we are waiting to see some kind  of continuing resolution if that passes  or  if we will have yet another government  shutdown next week thanks for watching  politics now you can stay up to date  online anytime and watch us here every  saturday night have a great night
 

